Subject: The collection trigger's threshold test is always inlined; the collection itself is out of line

What this does
  - `js_trigger_gc` splits into an always-inline threshold test (`malloc_size + size > malloc_gc_threshold`) and `js_trigger_gc_slow`, which holds the previous body unchanged (deferral, reconcile, collection, pacing).

Why we need it
  `js_trigger_gc` had one caller, so the compiler inlined it into JS_NewObjectFromShapeCapExtra. The dedicated allocator of 0440 is a second caller; the compiler then emitted `js_trigger_gc` out of line and every object allocation paid a call and the setup of its large body (+195M on rootpin, +35M on virt5 in the default kit, interpreter unaffected). With the test inline, rootpin and virt5 are below their numbers before 0439.

Soundness
  The same condition decides, and the same code runs when it holds; GC pacing is untouched. With FORCE_GC_AT_MALLOC the slow path is called unconditionally, as before.

--- a/quickjs.c
+++ b/quickjs.c
@@ -3200 +3200 @@
-static void js_trigger_gc(JSRuntime *rt, size_t size)
+static __attribute__((noinline)) void js_trigger_gc_slow(JSRuntime *rt, size_t size)
@@ -3255,0 +3256,11 @@
+/* The threshold test stays inline at every allocation site; the collection itself is out of line. */
+static inline __attribute__((always_inline)) void js_trigger_gc(JSRuntime *rt, size_t size)
+{
+#ifdef FORCE_GC_AT_MALLOC
+    js_trigger_gc_slow(rt, size);
+#else
+    if (unlikely((rt->malloc_state.malloc_size + size) > rt->malloc_gc_threshold))
+        js_trigger_gc_slow(rt, size);
+#endif
+}
+
